<p>Former Israeli prime ministers Naftali Bennett and Yair Lapid said on April 26 that they would merge their parties and run on a joint list in Israel’s election, expected later this year, in a direct effort to unseat Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu and unite a fragmented opposition. Multiple outlets said the new faction would be led by Bennett and branded as the “Together” party, while leaving room for former minister Gadi Eisenkot to join. The alliance brings together politicians from different parts of Israel’s opposition camp: Bennett, whom coverage described as right-wing, and Lapid, a centrist. The move matters because Israel’s opposition has remained divided, and both men cast the merger as an attempt to change the country’s direction after the crises that followed the October 7, 2023 attack and the wars since then. It also revives a partnership that formed a coalition government in 2021 before Netanyahu returned to power at the end of 2022.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/OcO6ibes6bPRx2sf5KcHvmPSlby-wojZsCXm0BjIZ7k03-Bu39MAM0QMhW71RGcLPfv2936KII6-2rF7Sg7UeRHgojm0J2bWQxanWFrnpwiNnylnml9mRbUj12oPNJeJzYOpFenb6zhIwGlFslbrGnQi_dH95a2KQ7J5fACSi2bXkza5jHmslYmh030M7AgFcuYjP9hXbMVADHol' alt='Naftali Bennett, left, and Yair Lapid on Sunday in Herzliya, Israel, where they said they would unite in a party to be called Yachad, Hebrew for “together.”' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Inquiry pledge: Bennett said that if elected, he would establish a national commission of inquiry into the failures leading up to the October 7, 2023 attack, an investigation the current Netanyahu government has rejected.</li><li>Trust message: During their joint rollout, Lapid vouched publicly for Bennett, saying, “Bennett is a right-wing politician, but an honest one, and there is trust between us,” underscoring the alliance’s effort to bridge ideological lines.</li><li>Shared branding: Bennett described the merger as the founding of the Beyahad, or “Together,” party under his leadership, while Lapid wrote on X that the move united what he called the “Repair Bloc”.</li><li>Election backdrop: Reuters-based coverage said the election is expected later this year, giving the merged list a short window to consolidate anti-Netanyahu voters and recruit additional figures such as Eisenkot.</li></ul><blockquote>We are standing here together for the sake of our ‌children. The State of Israel must change direction</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Naftali Bennett: Bennett cast the merger as a patriotic step and said the new party would &quot;lead to a great victory.&quot; (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1995320/two-former-israeli-pms-unite-to-unseat-netanyahu'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Yair Lapid: Lapid said the alliance would unite the &quot;Repair Bloc&quot; and focus its efforts on what he described as leading Israel toward needed repair. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1995320/two-former-israeli-pms-unite-to-unseat-netanyahu'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Media coverage described Bennett and Lapid as Netanyahu’s main rivals and framed the merger as an effort to overcome opposition fragmentation before the next vote. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/middle-east/article/3351517/netanyahus-biggest-rivals-join-forces-israels-coming-election'>South China Morning Post</a>, <a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/netanyahus-biggest-rivals-join-forces-israels-next-election'>Al-Monitor</a>, <a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/26/world/middleeast/israel-netanyahu-lapid-bennett.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/former-israeli-prime-ministers-agree-merge-parties-netanyahu-132398570'>Two former Israeli prime ministers join forces against Netanyahu in upcoming elections</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/04/26/two-former-israeli-prime-ministers-agree-to-merge-parties-against-netanyahu_6752855_4.html'>T